# Local runtime state, personal memory, generated workspaces
.vool_local/
.vool_stopped
.openclaw/
Beta_Website/
Beta_Website_Snapshots/
Beta_Website_Copies/
Beta_Website_Backups/

memory/
# ...but core/memory/ is PRODUCTION CODE, not runtime state. The rule above matches any
# directory named `memory` at any depth, so it catches it too. The four modules there
# survive only because they were tracked before this rule existed -- a NEW file added
# beside them is silently ignored, which is how work disappears (see rule 0.9).
!core/memory/
!core/memory/**
# The negation above out-ranks the global `__pycache__/` rule because it comes later, so
# build artefacts under it have to be re-ignored explicitly.
core/memory/__pycache__/
generated/
artifacts/acceptance_runs/
.verification-logs/
workspace/
# Recorded code-task journals keep the workspace bytes their tests recreate.
!tests/fixtures/code_task_journals/*/*/workspace/
sandbox_workspace/
control/
templates/
install_doctor.json
install_receipt.json
.worktrees/

# Explicit guardrail: internal handovers must stay local-only.
# Never put sensitive/operator handovers under docs/ or any tracked path.
# Match HANDOVER anywhere in the name (e.g. WINDOWS_AGENT_HANDOVER.md), not just a
# leading "HANDOVER_" — these files carry machine paths/usernames and must never ship.
*HANDOVER*.md
# HANDOFF is the same kind of file and the pattern above does not catch it. Found when a
# `git add -A` swept VOOL-DELIVERY/KAS_HANDOFF_CONTEXT_2026-08-02.md into a commit: one
# spelling was guarded and its synonym was not, so the guardrail depended on which word the
# author happened to pick.
*HANDOFF*.md
**/*HANDOVER*.md
HANDOVER_*.md
memory/HANDOVER_*.md
memory/INTERNAL_TAKEOVER*.md
memory/*TAKEOVER*.md
docs/HANDOVER_INTERNAL*.md
docs/INTERNAL_TAKEOVER*.md

# Personal / operator identity (never publish)
USER.md
IDENTITY.md
MEMORY.md
SOUL.md
AGENTS.md
HEARTBEAT.md
BOOTSTRAP.md
TOOLS.md
Cursor_Claude_Handover.md

# Handover docs (contain real node IPs, SSH paths, server configs)
docs/HANDOVER_2026-03-15_FULL_STATE.md
docs/HANDOVER_2026-03-14_HIVE_SURFACES_AND_RUNTIME_TRUTH.md

# Ops tooling is source: the verification gate itself lives here. Only caches are ignored
# (the old ops/* allowlist silently dropped required tooling from fresh checkouts).
ops/**/__pycache__/
# Restart-first-turn P0 (2026-09-02): the served-reality-style reproduction
# harness — loopback stub only, no IPs/secrets, must stay visible.
# R2 (AUD-20260829-003) static tree-wide check: no sys.modules[__name__] reassignment shims.
# Skip-accounting gate (recovered 2026-08-29 from audit/skip-accounting): a paired baseline
# runner and the controller that grades it. They exist on no other ref, so an `ops/*` miss
# here would lose them silently -- which is exactly what tests/test_ops_instruments_are
# _never_invisible.py now fails on.
# MILESTONE 0 boundary map (architecture convergence campaign): the rerunnable
# AST measurement of turn entrypoints / authority sites / import direction.
# Served-reality bench (P0, 2026-09-02): the exact-SHA release harness that
# stages one commit read-only and proves the assembled product over HTTP.
# Blast-radius selection + seeded hostile driver — loopback-only, no IPs/secrets.
# A DIRECTORY of instruments, so the negation has to cover the tree rather than name each
# file. `ops/*` above is an allowlist by design; a new tool that is not listed is invisible
# to `git status` and one `git clean` from gone -- which is what rule 0.9 exists to stop, and
# what happened when this directory was first added.
# Config-driven cluster launchers — pure glue (take --config <path>), no IPs/secrets.
# Without these negations the ops/* rule silently swallows them, so the bootstrap +
# cluster READMEs reference scripts that can never be committed.

# Build-time provenance stamp (written per build by installer/stamp_build_source.py)
config/build-source.json

